    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d617">

                <pb facs="0734" n="724" sameAs="frus:frus1948v08/pg724"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24 FLC/10–2848</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 447</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1948-10-28">October 28, 1948</date>.<lb/>[Received November
                        8.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: As authorized by the Department’s telegram
                    No. 1407, October 6, 7 p.m.,<anchor n="41" xml:id="d617-f1"/> repeated to the
                    American Consul General in Shanghai as 1724, the Minister-Counselor addressed a
                    letter to Dr. Tung Ling, Director, American Department of the Ministry of
                    Foreign Affairs, confirming the minutes of the agreement which had taken place
                    on September 21, 1948, regarding the Surplus Property Agreement. A <pb
                        facs="0735" n="725" xml:id="pg725"/> copy of that letter dated October 8 is
                    enclosed. There is also enclosed a copy of a reply from Dr. Tung Ling indicating
                    the approval of the Chinese Government of the tentative agreement reached on
                    September 21, and expressing the opinion that: “Since the approved minutes shall
                    be binding on both Governments, the letters exchanged between us will be
                    considered as adequate implementation of the Surplus Property Agreement”.</p>
                <p>I shall appreciate being informed whether the Department agrees that this
                    exchange of letters is all that is required to carry out what in Nanking we have
                    considered to be no alteration of the agreement, but merely agreed measures for
                    its implementation.<anchor n="42" xml:id="d617-f2"/></p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Ambassador:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lewis
                        Clark</hi></signed><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Minister-Counselor of
                        Embassy</hi></closer>



                <xi:include href="d617-a1.xml" xpointer="d617-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d617-a2.xml" xpointer="d617-a2"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d617-f1" n="41" xml:id="d617-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d617-f2" n="42" xml:id="d617-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram No. 1631, November 16, 6 p.m., the Department agreed that “exchange
                    of letters [was] sufficient” (893.24 FLC/11–1648).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
